00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.000
[Wren]: It's, uh, less saturated now.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.500
[Niko]: It's less saturated, and it's a little bit warmer.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.580
Look at the tone of the green of his eyes, the green on the rubber bands,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.080
but they're all in the same color space

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.980
In fact, the most saturated thing is his blue.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.500
And even then, the blue kind of rolls off to a desaturation as it gets brighter towards the edges of his head.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.080
It's much more accurately mimicking the color response of a camera.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.820
You need to obey the physical rules of your camera, with your CG footage, if you want it to look real.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.900
[Niko]: So, when we broke down the first Sonic trailer,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:24.400
[Wren]: 'Kay.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.520
[Niko]: we talked about the bloom from lightning.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.280
[Niko in ep.1]: The blue glow from the lighting, the camera would never capture it like that.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.940
Even though the camera is exposed way down to the point where the clouds aren't even white,

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.100
there's still glow coming through that electricity, which you wouldn't get.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.420
[Niko]: So, bloom happens when a light is so bright

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.660
that it diffuses as it goes through the lens of the camera,

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.440
and also as it hits some of the other elements as well.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.440
It's like, if you look at the old Sonic the Hedgehog trailer,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.840
there's a ton of bloom on that lightning

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.480
Now, if we look at the new Sonic, and we look at that lightning,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.420
the bloom is brought way more down.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.480
So, the lightning is bright,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.540
but it's not like it's any brighter than the Sun

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.560
or the sky, that's you know, giving us brightness behind it.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.340
In fact, you can kind of see the lightning over the sky right there,

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.900
and it's just a little bit brighter than the sky.

