[Niko]: I mean, they had CGI characters they had done,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.500
but this is the first one was a CGI character brought in-

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.440
[Wren]: into live-action photography, yeah.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.460
[Clint]: Dude! Oh, this is sick!

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.340
Oh, this is great! Like, they're not trying to make something that looked real.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.300
Dude, the way it jumps out of the stained-glass window is awesome.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.680
The way It's just like warping, and then it just *beeeesh*

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.340
[Clint]: That looks so cool!
[Wren]: And, also notice the motion blur.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.000
[Niko]: Yeah. Oh, that's right! They had motion blur, didn't they?
[Wren]: That was like brand-new technology at the time, too.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:05.860
[Wren]: Yeah.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.340
And, it was all just through coding where they're like,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.080
uhhh...

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.680
frame here, frame here, interpolate everything in between, and blur it out.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:11.860
[Clint]: Look at that, it's got shadows.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:12.640
[Niko]: Yeah.
[Clint]: It's got... It has shadows and everything.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:13.380
[Clint]: It's got... It has shadows and everything.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.700
[Wren]: His shadows, they weren't able to render the shadows back then.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:20.900
They didn't have any sort of system for shadows, so those shadows were actually hand animated

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:21.520
[Clint]: Yeah

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:22.360
[Wren]: into the scene.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.620
This was a groundbreaking visual effect at the time.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:27.460
This is actually pioneered by Dennis Muren and John Lasseter.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.140
Dennis Muren, who is a legend of visual effects.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.660
He has the most Academy Awards of any living person on the planet.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:35.160
[Niko]: Wow!

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.860
He was also the first visual effects artist to ever receive an Academy Award.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.700
[Niko]: And John Lasseter is the man who went on to found Pixar.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.460
[Wren]: Exactly. In fact, this scene right here-

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.620
They spent six months working on

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.220
the ten seconds of footage you see of the stained-glass man

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.280
They've pioneered a whole bunch of new rendering techniques.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.300
They actually, like, sculpted some of these pieces in clay

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.960
and used, uh, Polhemus 3SPACE...

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.160
...Digitizer.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.460
It's basically a wand

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.220
that you basically move it through 3D space,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.180
and it records point coordinates.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.260
[Clint]: Like a photo scanner thing?

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.040
[Wren]: It's a super rudimentary version that.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:05.680
You're literally just getting coordinates, though.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.280
[Niko]: Because, back in the day, when they're doing 3d animation, like,

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.460
there was no Autodesk, there was no Maya,

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:11.700
there was no Cinema 4D, there was no Blender.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:12.740
[Clint]: What the heck did they do?

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.540
[Niko]: They literally made it themselves.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.800
Like, they're using raw math.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.190
[Wren]: Yeah, they're doing, like, coding for this

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:18.240
[Clint]: Oh my god

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.100
[Niko]: You had to sit there,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.300
do the math for all the translations of all the vector points,

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.600
and then punch it into the computer, and wait a while,

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.660
and, hopefully, it looks okay

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:30.020
[Niko]: I mean, you can do 3D modeling with a pencil and paper by writing out X, Y, and Z coordinates,

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:30.420
[Wren]: Yeah

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:31.020
[Niko]: then plotting them.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.880
And that's to the point what they're doing here with that model.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.760
[Wren]: And John Lasseter actually painted all of what you see there.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.340
He painted the texture map for the knight.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:39.840
[Clint]: Sick!
He painted the texture map for the knight.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.300
So, ILM actually did the effects for this movie,

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.080
and, shortly after this movie came out,

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:46.560
it was a bit of a lackluster box office release.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.860
They actually sold that section of the company to Steve Jobs,

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.320
and that's what became Pixar.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:54.200
And, shortly after this, they did a whole short film that won like 30 awards,

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.420
and that went on to become, like, the very famous Pixar logo.

