00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:49.620
[Wren]: That tank is driving a building!

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.460
[Niko]: By the way, guys, it hasn't cut yet.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:52.880
[Wren]: Yeah, I did notice that.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.440
This is all one long take.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:56.060
We thought our Bosstown renders were long.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.820
[Niko]: The reason one take here is not trivial is because...

00:09:58.820 --> 00:09:59.760
Think about a video game.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.440
You can only have so many textures, so many characters on screen at the same time,

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:04.500
because there's only a certain amount of memory in your computer.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.500
When it comes to 3D animation, you can usually access more of your computer's memory,

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.629
but there's still only a limited amount of memory in your computer.

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:12.920
So, how do you have a world that has 100 characters in it,

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:14.400
1,000 buildings,

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.780
800 physics simulations with smoke and water and all that kind of stuff,

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:18.640
fountains all over the place,

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:20.080
clothing simulations all over the place.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.600
Like, how do you bring that all together in one shot?

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:23.140
And that's-

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:25.400
One of the most technically impressive things about this is that

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.520
it's element after element after element, all being set up individually,

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:31.200
and then being brought together in what looks like one long shot.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.920
Basically, artists have to go in,

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:38.140
and then, little by little, they build up pieces of it on like Individual simulations or setups or environments,

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:39.920
and they render them all out one by one.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.480
They just put all together layer by layer.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:44.420
[Captain Haddok]: Nice work, Snowy! Don't let him go!

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.300
[Niko]: Here's all the high-level things happening in this thing:

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.480
We have rigid body physics, AKA hard things that bounce off of each other.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:52.900
We have cloth physics.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:54.600
We have soft body physics, with the paper.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.660
We have...feather physics,

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:57.660
water, surface water.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:59.280
You have splashes, and you have foam.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:02.020
Foam simulation is another thing that's kind of crazy a lot people take for granted.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:04.140
Ragdolls as characters are being influenced by each other.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:08.730
We have hand animation and mocap animation that needs to drive physics.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:12.420
But one isn't physics and the other one is, so it's really easy to break things.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:13.340
[Clint]: Curry powder simulations?

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:14.840
[Niko]: We have curry powder simulation.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:16.200
[Wren]: Good boy simulation.

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:17.380
[Niko]: Good boy simulation.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:19.620
We have exhilarating story simulation...

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:21.320
