00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.220
[Wren]: It's very warm.
He has no blue light or blue edge.

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:39.460
[Niko]: Yeah, and then there's some just awkward light wrap being improperly used.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:42.340
So, light wrap: a really handy tool, a really common tool for green screening.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:44.760
Basically, it's to wrap some light from your background around your actor.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:45.620
The problem is...

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:47.880
...light wrap only makes sense when something behind you is bright.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.720
So, for example, the sky should be blooming or glowing,

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.480
and that should be the light wrap around his arm.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.980
But there's nothing bright on the bottom right or the bottom left, for that matter,

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.260
and we're still getting light wrap.

00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:01.440
It just really makes it feel like you're watching a bunch of filters being put on footage in After Effects

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:05.020
So, all those blue glaciers and pieces of ice rushing by,

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.000
especially the bright, white ones are reflecting a lot of light,

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.540
we should be seeing these colored lights, basically,

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.160
whooshing by Pierce Brosnan's face.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.260
And all you need to do is, on set, have, you know,

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:14.880
one of your gaffers take a light and kind of go like this

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:16.500
or, like, move a reflector around,

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.680
but there's none of that happening with Pierce Brosnan here.

